 ## LSP
 
 The Shape LSP validates `shape.toml` directly and provides completion, hover, and diagnostics for known sections and keys.
+
+## Checking, and Applying Fixes
+
+`shape check` type-checks a file or a project without executing anything:
+
+```sh
+shape check src/main.shape     # a single file
+shape check                    # the current directory, via shape.toml
+shape check --link             # additionally resolve every foreign symbol
+```
+
+Some diagnostics carry a **machine-applicable fix**: the compiler proves the
+exact byte spans and the exact replacement text, rather than describing a
+repair in prose. `shape check --fix` applies those, rewriting the checked file
+in place.
+
+A non-exhaustive match is the fix that ships today. Given this file:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+enum Status { Active, Inactive }
+
+fn describe(s: Status) -> string {
+    match s {
+        Status::Active => "on",
+    }
+}
+```
+
+`shape check` reports the missing variant, and `shape check --fix` writes the
+arm:
+
+```sh
+$ shape check --fix status.shape
+error: Semantic error: Non-exhaustive match on 'Status': missing variants Inactive
+  applied: Add missing match arm for Status
+fixed: applied 1 fix(es) to status.shape; re-run `shape check` to confirm
+```
+
+The arm arrives empty, because the compiler knows which variant is missing and
+does not know what you want it to do:
+
+```shape runnable=false
+    match s {
+        Status::Active => "on",
+        Status::Inactive => {
+        },
+    }
+```
+
+Fill it in and the program checks and runs:
+
+```shape
+enum Status { Active, Inactive }
+
+fn describe(s: Status) -> string {
+    match s {
+        Status::Active => "on",
+        Status::Inactive => "off",
+    }
+}
+
+print(describe(Status::Active))
+print(describe(Status::Inactive))
+```
+
+### What `--fix` will and will not do
+
+A fix is **evidence-backed**: it applies facts the compiler already proved —
+which variants a match is missing, what a scrutinee's enum is called — and
+never a guess. Where the proved facts do not determine a safe edit, the
+diagnostic still explains the problem and `--fix` offers nothing.
+
+A fix is also **bound to the revision it was proved against**. Each one carries
+a digest of the source it was checked on, so if the file changed between the
+check and the apply, the fix is refused rather than applied at offsets that no
+longer mean what they meant. You will see the diagnostic and an unmodified
+file, which is the correct outcome — re-run `shape check --fix` and get a fix
+proved against what you now have.
+
+Three limits worth knowing:
+
+- Only diagnostics that emit a fix can be fixed. Today that is the
+  non-exhaustive match; more are being added diagnostic by diagnostic.
+- The file is rewritten in place, with no backup and no preview. Run it on a
+  clean working tree.
+- A project is checked from its entry file, so `--fix` repairs that file and
+  not the modules it imports.
+
+The same proved fixes reach the editor as one-keystroke code actions, and the
+`--diagnostics json` output carries their spans verbatim, so a tool can apply
+them without running the compiler itself.
